-- title:
--   Splitting players, feasible coalitions and total balancedness of a constrained game
-- statement:
--   A game $(\tilde N,\tilde G)$ is **generated from** $(N,G)$ **by splitting players** when a splitting scheme $\Pi$ assigns to every $i\in N$ a nonempty set $\Pi(i)$ of subplayers, the sets $\Pi(i)$ are pairwise disjoint and cover $\tilde N$, and
--   $$
--   G(S)=\tilde G\Big(\bigcup_{i\in S}\Pi(i)\Big)\qquad(S\subseteq N).
--   $$
--   The **feasible coalitions** $\mathcal C$ are the sets $\Pi(S)=\bigcup_{i\in S}\Pi(i)$, and the **constrained game** $(\tilde N,\mathcal C,\tilde G)$ only imposes coalitional rationality on them. The constrained game is **totally balanced** when for every $S\subseteq N$ there is $y\in\mathbb R^{\tilde N}$ with
--   $$
--   \sum_{a\in\Pi(S)}y_a=\tilde G(\Pi(S)),\qquad \sum_{a\in\Pi(T)}y_a\le\tilde G(\Pi(T))\quad\text{for every }T\subseteq S.
--   $$
--
--   The PMS game is analysed by splitting each player into its jobs and then each job into unit-speed players; this definition makes those steps precise.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The splitting scheme is encoded by its father map $\pi:\tilde N\to N$ with $\Pi(i)=\pi^{-1}(i)$; conditions (i)–(iii) of Definition 1 say that $\pi$ is surjective. The paper uses "totally balanced" for constrained games without defining it; the definition above applies Definition 3 to each constrained subgame on a feasible coalition.
-- source:
--   Alon, Anily, The Basic Core of a Parallel Machines Scheduling Game, MSOM 25(6) (2023), doi:10.1287/msom.2021.0337, Definitions 1–3, p.2237 (PDF p.6)

import Mathlib

namespace SplitPMSGame

/-- **Feasible coalition** `∪_{i∈S} Π(i)` (Alon, Anily, *The Basic Core of a Parallel Machines
Scheduling Game*, MSOM 25(6) (2023), Definitions 1–2, p.2237): the subplayers descending from a
coalition `S ⊆ N` under the splitting scheme `Π`.

**Formalization Note** The splitting scheme `Π` (with `Π(i) ≠ ∅`, the `Π(i)` pairwise disjoint
and covering `Ñ`) is encoded by its father map `π : Ñ → N`, `Π(i) = π⁻¹{i}`; conditions (i)–(iii)
of Definition 1 say exactly that `π` is a surjective function. -/
def feasibleCoalition {Ñ N : Type*} [Fintype Ñ] [DecidableEq N] (π : Ñ → N) (S : Finset N) :
    Finset Ñ :=
  Finset.univ.filter (fun a => π a ∈ S)

/-- **The game generated by splitting** (MSOM 2023, Definition 1, p.2237): the characteristic
function `G(S) = G̃(∪_{i∈S} Π(i))` of the original game `(N, G)` from which `(Ñ, G̃)` is
generated by splitting players with the scheme `Π` (father map `π`); `Gt` is `G̃`. -/
def splitGame {Ñ N : Type*} [Fintype Ñ] [DecidableEq N] (π : Ñ → N) (Gt : Finset Ñ → ℝ) :
    Finset N → ℝ :=
  fun S => Gt (feasibleCoalition π S)

/-- **Totally balanced constrained game** `(Ñ, 𝒞, G̃)` (MSOM 2023, Definitions 2–3, p.2237).
The feasible coalitions `𝒞` are the sets `∪_{i∈S} Π(i)`, `S ⊆ N`. The constrained game is
balanced (Definition 3) when some cost allocation of `Ñ` is efficient and satisfies coalitional
rationality for every feasible coalition; it is totally balanced when this holds for every
constrained subgame: for every `S ⊆ N` there is `y : Ñ → ℝ` with
`∑_{a∈Π(S)} y_a = G̃(Π(S))` and `∑_{a∈Π(T)} y_a ≤ G̃(Π(T))` for every `T ⊆ S`.

**Formalization Note** The paper uses "totally balanced" for constrained games (Observation 1,
Theorem 3) without defining it; this is the reading in which the subgames of the constrained
game are those on feasible coalitions `Π(S)`, whose feasible subcoalitions are the `Π(T)`,
`T ⊆ S`. The splitting scheme is the father map `π` (`Π(i) = π⁻¹{i}`), and `Gt` is `G̃`. -/
def ConstrainedTotallyBalanced {Ñ N : Type*} [Fintype Ñ] [DecidableEq N] (π : Ñ → N)
    (Gt : Finset Ñ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ S : Finset N, ∃ y : Ñ → ℝ,
    ∑ a ∈ feasibleCoalition π S, y a = Gt (feasibleCoalition π S) ∧
      ∀ T ⊆ S, ∑ a ∈ feasibleCoalition π T, y a ≤ Gt (feasibleCoalition π T)

end SplitPMSGame


